WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Have you ever wondered
if there’s a natural way

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.160
to lower your high blood pressure,
guard against Alzheimer's,

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:11.440
lose weight, and feel better?
Well, it turns out there is!

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.680
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.040
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.800
You can’t buy an
epigenetic clock,

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.760
but you can use one to measure
the age of your tissues and cells

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.560
and to predict health outcomes
and life expectancy.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:27.360
In our first story,

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.640
we look at how epigenetics
have surpassed telomere length

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:33.840
as the best age predictor.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.960
Epigenetics—the differential
expression of genes—

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.160
both establishes the character
and function of a cell

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.440
and maintains that identity
over time

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.960
through round after round
of cell division.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.320
So, a heart cell
stays a heart cell

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.200
and divides
to make more heart cells,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.240
instead of skin cells
or kidney cells,

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.480
even though all our main cells

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.520
have the same
entire complement of DNA

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.720
to potentially be anything.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:00.920
This is accomplished
by methylation,

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.880
chemical markers that silence
inappropriate genes

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:05.080
in a particular cell.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:06.840
The fidelity
of that maintenance

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.000
of methylation is good-
97 percent to 99.9 percent

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.720
every division-but not perfect.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:17.120
Over time, those tiny errors
may add up

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.640
and may help explain why
the methylation patterns

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.680
of identical twins
drift apart as they age.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:26.520
The epigenetic markers
of young identical twins

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.280
are essentially
indistinguishable

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:29.920
but then diverge over time.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.440
Identical twins have
the same DNA, the same genes,

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.920
but the difference
in gene expression

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.280
among older
identical twin pairs

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.040
were found to be
about four times greater

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.720
than those observed
in young pairs.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:48.360
This may result in them each
getting different diseases.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.960
An age-related disease
like Alzheimer's

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.480
only has an identical twin
concordance rate

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.920
of about 50 percent,
meaning if one twin gets it,

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.920
there's only
about a coin flip chance

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.560
that the other will too,
despite identical DNA.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.800
Or, even if they do
both get it,

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.520
the disease may manifest
decades apart.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.280
Any epigenetic differences
that may contribute

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:11.720
to differential disease rates

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.800
may arise from having
different diets and lifestyles

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.960
or may be a result
of random epigenetic drift.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:20.640
However, there are
certain DNA sites

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.400
on our chromosomes
that predictably methylate

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.360
or demethylate as we age.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:28.920
So predictable, in fact,
it's like clockwork.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.080
One of the earliest attempts
to study aging

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.880
and the epigenome

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.960
(which is only
like a dozen years ago)

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.440
found that the DNA
from a 103-year-old

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.680
appeared to be
less methylated overall

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.960
than the DNA
of a newborn infant,

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.000
suggesting, perhaps,
that aging involves

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.480
the general loss
of epigenetic markings.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.000
We now know
it's more complicated than that.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.080
Of the methylation sites
that reliably change as we age,

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.440
about 60 percent go
from methylated to unmethylated,

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.800
and the other 40 percent become
more methylated over time.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.080
Some so reliably change
with age

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.040
that they've been considered

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.920
a "molecular crystal ball
for human aging."

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.520
In a remarkable triumph
of Big Data,

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.440
out of the millions
of methylation sites

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:13.680
in our DNA,

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:17.560
a tiny subset
so dependably shift over time

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.160
that you can predict
someone's age

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:20.480
within a few years

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.800
just by strategically measuring
the methylation pattern

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.800
in a few hundred
or even just a few dozen sites

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:30.040
in someone's
three-billion-letter genome.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:31.400
Over just the last few years,

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:34.000
these "epigenetic clocks"
have become established

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.480
as robust measures
of chronological age,

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.760
surpassing telomere length
as the best age predictor.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:41.920
Who cares, though?

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:45.000
I mean, why invent some costly
Rube Goldberg approach

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.280
to divining someone's age
when you can simply ask them?

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:51.160
Well, you can imagine
forensic applications,

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:53.760
the determination
of an unidentified victim's age

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:55.400
with a blood sample,

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:58.080
but that's just scratching
the surface.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.000
The kicker is
that epigenetic clocks

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:04.080
don't just track
your chronological age

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:07.000
but appear to measure
your true biological age.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:08.760
In other words,
your epigenetic age

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.760
can better predict
your remaining life expectancy

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.000
than your calendar age.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:15.760
It's like science fiction.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:18.560
Feed a drop of blood
into some futuristic machine

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.680
that scans the placement
of chemical markers

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:23.760
on a strand of DNA,
and it spits out your true age,

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.160
reflecting a lifetime
of lifestyle choices.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:28.440
If the machine calculated

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.320
that you have
the DNA methylation pattern

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.440
of a 60-year-old, but you've
only had 50 birthdays,

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:38.000
that would be an example of
"epigenetic age acceleration,"

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.200
when your epigenetic clock age
is older

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.600
than your actual
chronological age.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:46.320
That would be an indication
that you're aging too fast.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:47.880
As a 50-year-old,

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.720
you'd think you have another,
mm, 30 years on this Earth,

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.640
but because
the epigenetic clock shows

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:54.760
that you're aging
at such an accelerated pace,

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:58.280
it's more like you only have
another 20 years left.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:01.440
Every five years
of epigenetic age acceleration

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:02.640
is associated

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:06.360
with an 8 to 15 percent
increased risk of mortality.

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:09.440
In addition to predicting
time-to-death,

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:11.920
epigenetic clocks
also appear to foretell

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:15.040
healthspan indicators,
such as cognitive decline,

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:18.680
frailty, arthritis,
and the progression of diseases

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:21.360
like Alzheimer's
and Parkinson's.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:22.160
As you can imagine,

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.400
the insurance agency*
has jumped on this,

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.400
and your premiums
may soon be determined

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.520
by your epigenetic age.

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:31.240
But it's not some
Gypsy fortune teller curse

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:32.520
set in stone.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:34.720
You can change the rate
at which you age

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:37.240
and may soon be able
to use epigenetic clocks

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:39.040
to track your progress,

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:41.000
potentially presenting
a radically faster

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:45.960
and cheaper way to test
anti-aging interventions.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:46.680
In our next story,

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:52.680
we look at how a baby with
a heavy surrogate mother

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:58.520
and a thin biological mom may harbor
a greater risk of becoming obese

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:03.080
than a baby with a slim surrogate
mom and a heavy biological one.

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:07.920
Identical twins don't just share
DNA; they also shared a uterus.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:11.920
Might that also help account for some
of their metabolic similarities?

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:16.240
Fetal overnutrition, evidenced by
an abnormally large birth weight,

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:21.640
seems to be a strong predictor of
obesity in childhood and later in life.

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:24.960
Could it be you are what your mom ate?

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.600
A dramatic illustration from the
animal world is the cross breeding

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:32.800
of Shetland ponies with massive
draft horses. I mean, either way,

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:37.080
the offspring are half pony, half
horse, but in the pony uterus

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:40.920
they come out much smaller—
thank heavens for the poor pony.

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:44.880
This is presumably the same reason
why the mule—donkey dad and mare—

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:49.120
is larger than the hinny—
stallion and donkey mom.

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:53.120
The way you test this in people
is to study the size of babies

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:57.000
from surrogate mothers
after in vitro fertilization.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.920
Who do you think most determines
the birth weight of a test-tube baby:

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.720
the donor mom who
provided all the DNA

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:08.240
or the surrogate mom who provided
the intrauterine environment?

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:12.120
When it was put to the test,
the womb won.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:15.440
Incredibly, a baby born to
an obese surrogate mother

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:20.320
with a skinny biological mom may
harbor a greater risk of becoming obese

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:24.840
than a baby from a big biological
mom born to a slim surrogate.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:28.000
The researchers conclude the
environment provided by the mother

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:32.760
is more important than her
genetic contribution to birth weight.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:36.320
The most compelling data comes from
comparing obesity rates in siblings

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:41.600
born to the exact same mother before
and after her bariatric surgery.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:45.240
Compared to their brothers and
sisters born before the surgery,

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:49.560
those born when mom weighed about
100 pounds less had lower rates

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:52.960
of inflammation, metabolic
derangements, and most critically,

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:56.760
three times less risk
of developing severe obesity,

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:59.960
afflicting 35% of those born
before the weight loss,

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:02.640
compared to 11% born after.

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:06.440
The researchers conclude these
data emphasize how critical it is

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:09.680
to prevent obesity and treat
it effectively to prevent

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:12.960
further transmission
to future generations.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:16.960
But wait. Mom had the same
DNA before and after surgery.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:19.320
She passed the
same genes down.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:24.720
How could her weight during
pregnancy affect the weight destiny

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:29.560
of her children any differently?
Darwin himself admitted

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:33.400
that the greatest error he
committed had been not allowing

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:37.240
sufficient weight to the direct action
of the environment, like food,

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:39.560
independently of natural selection.

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:46.520
We finally figured out the mechanism
by which this can happen: epigenetics.

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:50.560
Epigenetics—literally meaning above
genetics—layers an extra level

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:55.400
of information on top of the DNA
sequence that can be both affected

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:59.440
by our surrounds and potentially
passed on to our children.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.000
This is thought to explain the
developmental programming

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:05.720
that can occur in the womb
depending on the weight

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:09.040
of the mother, or even
your grandmother.

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:12.720
Since all the eggs in your infant
daughters' ovaries are already

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:14.720
preformed before birth,

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:17.480
a mother's weight status during
pregnancy could potentially affect

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:22.240
the obesity risk of her grandchildren
too. Either way, you can imagine

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:25.680
how this could result in an
intergenerational vicious cycle

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:28.520
where obesity begets obesity.

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:30.840
Is there anything
we can do about it?

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:34.960
Well, breastfed infants may be
at lower risk for later obesity,

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:38.560
though the benefits may be
confined to exclusive breastfeeding,

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:40.680
as the effect may be
due to growth factors

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:44.400
triggered by exposure to the
excess protein in baby formula.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:46.880
The breastfeeding data
is controversial though,

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:50.320
with charges leveled
of a white hat bias.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:53.720
That's the concern that public health
researchers might disproportionally

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:58.360
shelve research results that doesn't
fit into some goal for the greater good—

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:01.280
in this case preferably
publishing breastfeeding studies

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:04.440
showing more positive results—
but of course that's coming from

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:08.120
someone who works for
an infant formula company.

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:11.720
Breast is best regardless;
its role in the childhood obesity

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:15.160
epidemic just remains
arguably uncertain.

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:18.760
Prevention may be the key.
Given the epigenetic influence

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:21.920
of maternal weight during
pregnancy, a symposium

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:25.160
of experts on pediatric nutrition
concluded that planning

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:28.960
of pregnancy, including prior
optimization of maternal weight

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:32.520
and metabolic condition,
offers a safe means to initiate

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:37.160
the prevention rather than
treatment of pediatric obesity.

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:41.520
Easier said than done, but overweight
moms-to-be may take comfort

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:44.640
in the fact that after the weight
loss in the surgery study

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:49.320
even the moms who gave birth to kids
with three times lower risk were still,

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:52.400
on average, obese themselves,
suggesting weight loss

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:56.960
before pregnancy is not
an all-or-nothing proposition.

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:58.000
Finally today,

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:02.120
how the anti-inflammatory and antioxidant
properties of plant-based diets

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:05.840
may explain why they can
effectively reverse cellular aging

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:08.640
by elongating telomeres.

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:12.040
Identifying simple strategies
to prevent or delay age-related

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:15.040
diseases is a major
public health concern.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:18.520
But how could you measure
the effects of such strategies?

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:22.920
Well, telomere length is a reliable
hallmark of biological aging

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:26.276
and the risk of developing
age-related chronic diseases.

00:11:26.328 --> 00:11:27.560
What is a telomere,

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:30.760
and why does it matter
how long they are?

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:36.640
Telomere comes from the Greek for
"end" "part" of our chromosomes.

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:39.000
Telomeres cap the ends
of our chromosomes

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:42.560
like shoelace tips to essentially
keep our DNA from fraying.

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:46.400
Telomere length is important since
there's a minimum length required,

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:50.800
but every time our cells divide,
a bit of the telomere is lost

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:54.200
and once they get too short,
the cell can die.

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:58.480
That's why telomeres are sometimes
called the molecular clock of cells.

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:02.560
Every year they get shorter and
shorter, kind of like life's fuse,

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:06.640
but in some people that fuse
burns faster than in others.

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:10.440
Accelerated telomere shortening
has been identified as a key biomarker

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:14.280
for accelerated aging, disease risk,
and diminished longevity.

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:16.480
But there's some good news.

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:19.200
Telomere shortening can be
counteracted by an enzyme

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.400
in our cells called telomerase.

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:25.960
Telomerase can replenish the lost
bits and elongate our telomeres.

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:31.560
So how can we boost this enzyme
to, in effect, reverse cellular aging?

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:33.840
Exercise may help.
Those with high levels

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:36.040
of physical activity
have longer telomeres,

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:40.920
whereas obese individuals and smokers
tend to have shorter telomeres,

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:43.480
along with those getting
inadequate sleep.

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:45.720
But what about nutrition?

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:49.760
Globally, we might expect that any
antioxidant or anti-inflammatory diet

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:52.040
could be protective for telomeres.

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:54.560
So we're talking like
a whole food plant-based diet

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:58.400
with a reduced intake of meat and,
in fact, swapping out animal protein

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:01.840
in general in favor
of plant-based proteins.

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:04.920
Given that plant-based foods
have well-known antioxidant

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:08.520
and anti-inflammatory effects,
there are fair grounds to believe

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:10.240
that the consumption
of plant-based foods

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:12.960
can help to counteract
telomere attrition,

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:18.640
but you don't know if it actually
would, until you put it to the test.

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:21.640
Dr. Dean Ornish, along
with the Nobel laureate

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:24.080
who co-discovered
the telomerase enzyme,

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:26.720
studied the effects of
comprehensive lifestyle changes

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:29.200
on telomerase activity
and telomere length

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:31.840
using the same plant-based
diet and lifestyle program

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:34.520
shown to reverse the progression
of heart disease and

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:39.240
early-stage prostate cancer, and
maybe even early-stage Alzheimer's.

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:42.400
And telomere length shortened
in the control group,

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:44.840
and they aged five years
as expected,

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:47.880
but didn't just not shorten
as much or hold steady,

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:50.920
but actually lengthened in
the plant-based lifestyle group.

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:53.680
Whereas in a similar study
across a similar time frame,

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:56.800
there was no difference in
telomere length when just giving

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:00.480
people the more typical low-fat dairy,
skinless chicken breast,

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:03.400
generic-type
healthier dietary advice.

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:07.320
Antioxidant-rich plant foods
help maintain telomere length.

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:10.440
In contrast, total and saturated fat
intake and consumption

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:12.920
of refined flour grains,
meat and meat products,

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:15.760
and soda relate
to shorter telomeres.

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:17.880
People eating more
anti-inflammatory diets

00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:20.000
tend to have longer telomeres,

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:23.280
and the greater the anti-inflammatory
potential of the diet over time,

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:25.840
the greater potential
to significantly slow down

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:28.480
the rate of telomere shortening.

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:30.720
Those with the most
pro-inflammatory diets

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:34.800
had almost twice the risk of
accelerated telomere shortening.

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:38.600
The most pro-inflammatory
food component is saturated fat,

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:42.880
found in meat, dairy, eggs and junk,
along with other pro-inflammatory

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:46.320
food components like
cholesterol and trans fat.

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:49.360
Omega-3's tend to be
anti-inflammatory,

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:52.640
but when put to the test,
fish oil supplements

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:55.880
failed to have any
significant telomere effects.

00:14:55.880 --> 00:14:59.400
The most anti-inflammatory
food component is fiber.

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:02.560
And indeed, if you look at dietary
fiber intake and telomere length

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:06.120
in a representative sampling
of thousands of US adults,

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:08.920
even though nobody
was eating enough,

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:10.840
the more fiber people consumed,

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:13.880
the longer their telomeres
tended to be.

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:16.600
Since there appeared
to be a straight-line increase,

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:17.880
they could do the math,

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:22.400
and it appeared that just a 10-gram
increase in fiber per 1000 calories

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:26.640
would equate to four fewer years
of biologic aging,

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:28.560
whereas, for example,
the consumption of soda

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:30.400
appeared to increase cell aging

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:33.467
by almost two years
per daily serving.

